Develop a theory of motivic integration on Artin stacks that extends the classical theory of motivic integration on schemes (and Deligne–Mumford stacks) in the following sense. Recall that the classical theory associates to a smooth variety X over a field of characteristic zero its arc space L(X), equipped with a measure taking values in (a completion of) the Grothendieck ring of varieties, and provides a change-of-variables formula along proper birational morphisms Y → X expressed in terms of the relative canonical divisor. The goal is to construct, for a suitable class of Artin stacks 𝒳 over a field, the following data:

(i) a notion of arc space (or arc stack) L(𝒳) of 𝒳;
(ii) a motivic measure μ_𝒳 on (constructible / measurable subsets of) L(𝒳), taking values in a suitable ring of motivic invariants;
(iii) a motivic integral ∫_{L(𝒳)} 𝕃^{-ord(I)} dμ_𝒳 for ideal sheaves (or analogous data) I on 𝒳;
(iv) a change-of-variables formula along appropriate morphisms 𝒴 → 𝒳 of Artin stacks involving a relative canonical class.

The theory must be of sufficient strength to yield McKay-correspondence type statements: namely, given a finite (or linearly reductive) group G acting on a smooth variety V with quotient X = V/G and stack quotient 𝒳 = [V/G], the stringy/orbifold invariants of X should be recovered as motivic integrals on L(𝒳), and more generally, crepant resolutions of singular varieties by smooth Artin stacks should produce equalities of stringy E-functions or motivic integrals via the change-of-variables formula.

Construct such a theory, or prove that no such extension with these properties exists.

